		return Date{}, &InvalidDateError{Value: s}
	}
	year, ok := parseDigits(s[0:4]); ...
}
```

parseDigits: each byte '0'-'9', accumulate.

UnmarshalJSON:

```go
func (d *Date) UnmarshalJSON(data []byte) error {
	if len(data) < 2 || data[0] != '"' || data[len(data)-1] != '"' {
		return &InvalidDateError{Value: string(data)}
	}
	var s string
	if err := json.Unmarshal(data, &s); err != nil {
		return &InvalidDateError{Value: string(data)}
	}
	parsed, err := ParseDate(s)
	if err != nil {
		return err
	}
	*d = parsed
	return nil
}
```

Note: ParseDate error would have Value = decoded string. That's fine — ErrInvalidDate recognizable.

Edge: `"\u0032025-01-10"` → decoded "2025-01-10" → valid. `"\u002b1"` → "+1" → ParseDate fails since '+' not digit. Good.

Also should the raw text check remain? json.Unmarshal on `"..."` handles invalid escapes → error → InvalidDateError. Good.

Check store.go for corrupt state handling to make sure nothing else needed.

